# cli-todo-app

A command-line todo list for Node.js. Todos live in `todos.json` in the directory you run the command from. There are no runtime dependencies.

## Requirements

Node.js 22 or later.

## Usage

```console
$ node index.js add "Buy groceries"
Added todo 1: Buy groceries

$ node index.js add Walk the dog          # quotes are optional
Added todo 2: Walk the dog

$ node index.js complete 1
Completed todo 1: Buy groceries

$ node index.js list
1. [x] Buy groceries
2. [ ] Walk the dog

2 todos: 1 done, 1 pending

$ node index.js delete 2
Deleted todo 2: Walk the dog
```

| Command | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `add <text>` | Adds a pending todo. Several unquoted words are joined with single spaces. |
| `list` | Prints every todo as `<id>. [x] <text>` (done) or `<id>. [ ] <text>` (pending), sorted by id, then a summary line. An empty list prints a hint instead. |
| `complete <id>` | Marks the todo done. Completing a todo that is already done prints a note and exits 0. |
| `delete <id>` | Removes the todo. |
| `help`, `--help`, `-h` | Prints usage. |

Output goes to stdout; errors go to stderr as a single `Error: ...` line. Usage errors are followed by the usage text.

### Exit codes

| Code | Meaning | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | Success | Any successful command, including `list` on an empty list |
| 1 | The command could not be carried out | No todo with that id; `todos.json` is corrupt or unreadable; lock timeout |
| 2 | The command was called incorrectly | No command, unknown command, missing or extra arguments, an id like `abc` or `0`, empty or too-long text, a malformed `TODO_*` variable |

### Configuration

| Variable | Default | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TODO_MAX_TEXT_LENGTH` | `500` | Maximum todo length, in Unicode code points |
| `TODO_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS` | `5000` | How long a write waits for another command to release the lock |
| `TODO_STALE_LOCK_MS` | `10000` | Age after which a leftover lock file is treated as abandoned and removed |

Each must be a positive whole number. Any other value is rejected with exit code 2; it is not silently ignored.

## Storage format

```json
{
  "nextId": 4,
  "todos": [
    { "id": 1, "text": "Buy groceries", "completed": true, "createdAt": "2026-09-30T11:47:02.261Z" },
    { "id": 3, "text": "Call mom", "completed": false, "createdAt": "2026-09-30T11:47:03.393Z" }
  ]
}
```

Every todo has exactly the four required fields: `id` (integer), `text`, `completed` (boolean), `createdAt` (ISO 8601). `nextId` is the id the next `add` will use.

A file that holds a bare array of todos is also accepted, so a hand-written or older file still works. It is rewritten in the object format on the next change.

## Design decisions

### Module layout

| File | Responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `index.js` | Entry point. Passes `process.argv` to `run`, sets the exit code, and handles process-level concerns such as a closed stdout pipe. |
| `src/cli.js` | Argument checks, command dispatch, turning errors into messages and exit codes. `run(argv, options)` accepts `cwd`, `env`, `stdout`, `stderr`, and `now`, so tests can drive it in-process. |
| `src/todos.js` | Pure state operations (`addTodo`, `completeTodo`, `deleteTodo`) and input validation (`normalizeText`, `parseId`). No I/O. Each operation returns a new state object and leaves its input untouched. |
| `src/storage.js` | Reading, validating, and atomically writing `todos.json`, plus the lock. |
| `src/format.js` | Rendering `list` output and escaping control characters. |
| `src/config.js`, `src/errors.js` | Environment-driven limits; error classes that carry their exit code. |

### Id allocation

There were two reasonable ways to pick the next id:

1. **Highest existing id + 1.** This needs no extra state, but it reuses an id when you delete the newest todo. Delete todo 3, add a new one, and it also becomes 3, so an old `complete 3` in your shell history now acts on the wrong item.
2. **A persisted counter (`nextId`)** that only goes up.

This app uses the counter, so ids are never reused. Approach 1 survives as the fallback for files that have no `nextId` (bare arrays), and loading always raises `nextId` above the highest existing id. A hand-edited counter therefore can't produce duplicates.

### Writes are atomic and serialized

- **Atomic replace.** A write goes to a uniquely named temp file in the same directory, is flushed with `fsync`, then renamed over `todos.json`. A crash mid-write leaves the old file intact, and `list` never sees half a file.
- **Lock file.** `add`, `complete`, and `delete` read, modify, and write the file. Two commands running at once could otherwise both read the same state and one update would be lost. Each of these commands holds `todos.json.lock` for the whole cycle. The lock is created with an exclusive-create flag, which makes creation an atomic test-and-set. Waiting commands retry every 20 ms until `TODO_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS`. `list` does not take the lock, since the atomic rename already guarantees it reads a complete file.
- **Abandoned locks.** A lock older than `TODO_STALE_LOCK_MS` is assumed to belong to a process that was killed mid-command, and is removed. Detection is by file age only, which is best-effort: if two waiting processes both judge the same lock stale at the same moment, one could remove the other's fresh lock. The window is a few milliseconds, and it only opens after a crash. The lock also assumes a local filesystem; exclusive create is not reliable on some network filesystems.

The test suite covers this directly: it launches parallel `add` processes and parallel `complete`/`delete` processes, then checks that none of the updates were lost.

### Input rules

- **Text** is trimmed and must be non-empty. It may not contain control characters (newlines, tabs, ESC, and so on). That keeps `list` to one line per todo and stops text from injecting terminal escape sequences. Length is counted in code points, so an emoji counts as one character, not two UTF-16 units.
- **Ids** must be decimal digits forming a safe integer ≥ 1. `1.5`, `1e2`, `0x1`, `-1`, `+1`, and non-ASCII digits are rejected. Leading zeros are allowed (`007` is 7).
- **Command names** are looked up in a `Map`, so inputs like `constructor` or `__proto__` are unknown commands, not object-prototype properties.
- Invalid input is rejected before the lock is taken or the file is read, so a bad argument gets a precise message even when the file is busy or damaged.

### Damaged files are never overwritten

If `todos.json` is not valid JSON or fails schema validation, every command stops with exit code 1. The message names the file and the problem, such as `todo at index 2 (id 5) has an invalid "completed"`. The file is not rewritten, so nothing is lost; fix it or move it aside. An empty file and a UTF-8 byte order mark are both tolerated. Unknown extra fields on a todo are kept on rewrite.

When `list` prints text from a hand-edited file that contains control characters, it shows them as visible `\uXXXX` escapes.

### Isolation

Each directory has its own `todos.json` and its own lock, so separate projects never share state or wait on each other.

## Development

```console
$ npm install        # dev dependencies (ESLint) only
$ npm test           # node --test "test/**/*.test.js"
$ npm run lint       # eslint .
$ npm run check      # lint, then test
```

Tests use the built-in `node:test` runner and create a fresh temp directory for each case.

| File | Level | Covers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `test/todos.test.js` | Unit | State operations, text and id validation, no mutation of inputs |
| `test/format.test.js` | Unit | `list` rendering, alignment, ordering, escaping |
| `test/config.test.js` | Unit | Environment parsing and rejection of bad values |
| `test/storage.test.js` | Integration | Load/save round trips, schema validation, atomic writes, lock timeout and stale-lock recovery, permission errors |
| `test/cli.test.js` | Integration | Each command through `run()` against a real directory, help, and error reporting |
| `test/e2e.test.js` | End-to-end | Spawns `node index.js` for the full lifecycle, exit codes, stream separation, and closed-pipe handling |
| `test/edge-cases.test.js` | Edge cases | Missing or extra arguments, invalid and unknown ids, unicode, length limits, corrupt files, prototype-key commands, concurrent processes |

## Out of scope

Editing a todo's text, un-completing, priorities, due dates, acting on several ids at once, and a custom file location. `list` shows every todo; there are no filters.

